## Changes from upstream

- The comment on the URL password rule in `patterns.ts` is reworded.
- The marker is renamed to `[JANITOR-REDACTED:<kind>]`: the `mark` function and the `MARK` pattern.
- `MARK` is anchored, so a value counts as a marker only when it is one whole. A global `MARKS` pattern is used to judge the rest of a partly redacted value.
- The URL password rule allows a slash in the password. A value of up to 5 digits followed by `/`, or by a `?` query with an `=`, is skipped as a port. The user part may contain whole markers but no other `[`. A password cannot contain `://`.
- The scheme of the URL password rule is matched by a lookahead and lookbehind at the `://` (`(?=:\/\/)(?<=\b[a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*)(:\/\/)`), not by a prefix `\b([a-z][a-z0-9+.-]*:\/\/)`. The prefix scanned a whole run of scheme characters from every word boundary, which was quadratic (16 KB of `A1-` took 0.5-1.4 s). The URLs that are hidden are the same, with a scheme of any length; the rule's first capture group is now `://`.
- The port skip of the URL password rule scans a query for its `=` with `(?:(?!:\/\/)[^\s@#])*=`, not `[^\s@#]*=`, so the scan ends at the next `://`. For every `://` whose password starts with digits then `?`, the plain class scanned on past every later `://` and past any `=`, up to the next whitespace, `@` or `#`, which was quadratic on a long line without them. The URLs that are hidden are the same: a password cannot contain `://`.
- The 22 token rules of upstream's `TOKENS` list in `patterns.ts` are moved, unchanged, into files under `rules/` by category: `rules/key-material.ts` (private-key, jwt, bearer-token), `rules/cloud.ts` (aws-access-key, the two aws-secret-key rules, aws-session-token, google-api-key), `rules/code-hosts.ts` (the two github-token rules), `rules/ai.ts` (anthropic-key, the three openai-key rules, huggingface-token), `rules/messaging-payments.ts` (slack-token, slack-webhook, the two stripe-key rules) and `rules/dev-services.ts` (npm-token, pypi-token, sendgrid-key). The `Rule` type is moved from `patterns.ts` to `rules/rule.ts`. The files are the janitor's; the rules in them are upstream's. `patterns.ts` still holds `TOKENS`, module-private, listing the rules by name in upstream's order, and exports `TOKEN_ORDER` (each rule's kind and pattern source) for the order test.
- The tests in `tests/patterns.test.ts` are changed to match these, and extended.
- `KNOWN-MISSES.md` and `tests/known-misses.test.ts` are the janitor's own: the first lists the secrets the shield knowingly does not hide, the second pins each entry.
- The slash command is renamed to /janitor-secret-shield.

A future upstream sync is a manual merge. The sed file at `reports_dev/secret-shield/upstream-sync.sed.txt` covers only the marker rename.

## Rules taken from other projects

- `slackAppToken`, `slackConfigAccessToken` and `slackConfigRefreshToken` in `rules/messaging-payments.ts` are taken word for word from the gitleaks rules slack-app-token, slack-config-access-token and slack-config-refresh-token (https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks, release v8.30.1, MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2019 Zachary Rice). The only change is that the leading `(?i)` is written as the scoped group `(?i:...)`.
- `googleOAuthAccessToken` in `rules/cloud.ts` is copied word for word from veil's rule for a Google OAuth access token (`ya29.`) (the pattern text; veil compiles it with the flags `gd`, here `g`). `googleOAuthClientSecret` and `googleOAuthRefreshToken` are adapted from veil's client secret and refresh token (`1//0`) rules (https://github.com/goncaloneves/veil, commit 0e57a35adc8abc68ab97f7b8d331bfdd66eec2c6, version v0.2.6, MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2026 Gonçalo Neves). The client secret adaptation changes the tail `{28}` plus the end guard `(?![\w-])` to `{20,}` with no end guard. The refresh token adaptation adds `+` to the lookbehind, `(?<![\w/])` becoming `(?<![\w/+])`. Veil's entropy floor, keyword prefilter and shape allowlist (`veil.ts` lines 91-100) are not carried over, so these rules hide more than veil reports.
- `digitaloceanPat`, `digitaloceanOAuthToken` and `digitaloceanRefreshToken` in `rules/cloud.ts` are adapted from the gitleaks rules digitalocean-pat, digitalocean-access-token (named digitalocean-oauth-token here) and digitalocean-refresh-token (cmd/generate/config/rules/digitalocean.go, https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks, release v8.30.1, MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2019 Zachary Rice). The changes: the `utils.GenerateUniqueTokenRegex` wrapper (a leading boundary and a trailing terminator) is dropped, the body `{64}` becomes `{64,}`, and the rules are case-insensitive for the whole token (upstream: the refresh token rule only).
- The 13 GitLab rules in `rules/code-hosts.ts` (`gitlabPat` to `gitlabFeatureFlagClientToken`) are adapted from the gitleaks rules in cmd/generate/config/rules/gitlab.go (https://github.com/gitleaks/gitleaks, release v8.30.1, MIT licence, Copyright (c) 2019 Zachary Rice); the prefixes are checked against docs.gitlab.com/security/tokens, and the routable length against GitLab merge request 169322. Kinds renamed: gitlab-ptt to gitlab-trigger-token, gitlab-rrt to gitlab-runner-registration-token, gitlab-kubernetes-agent-token to gitlab-agent-token, gitlab-runner-authentication-token to gitlab-runner-token. Kinds merged: gitlab-pat-routable into gitlab-pat, and gitlab-runner-authentication-token-routable into gitlab-runner-token (one rule each, with the optional routable tail). Not taken: gitlab-session-cookie (a known defect, KNOWN-MISSES.md). The changes: the fixed body lengths (20, 25, 50, 64) become `{20,}`; the glptt- body `[0-9a-f]{40}` becomes the shared body; the boundaries (`\b` and the trailing terminator) are dropped; a lookahead is added that requires a digit or an upper-case letter inside the body; the prefixes are case-insensitive letter by letter and the rules have no `i` flag; and glwt- and glrtr-, which gitleaks has no rule for, are added from the GitLab documentation with the shared body.
